    {
      "predicate": "iadas:authorConclusion",
      "object": "Bodybuilding may represent a practice that encourages male to become more self-conscious of their bodies, putting them at greater risk of experiencing greater levels of monitoring of body appearance and anxiety about physical appearance. This body self consciousness is associated with a greater risk of body dissatisfaction and drive for muscularity. Weightlifting does not appear to represent an objectifying climate."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:limites",
      "object": "The cross sectional nature makes it possible only to speculate on the directions of causality. Unequal sample sizes of groups in our analyses, particularly with respect to fewer competitive weightlifters. Unknown selection bias that may be operating in the bodybuilder and weightlifter groups, and any subsequent influence on the interpretation of our results. The weighlifting and bodybuilder groups differed not only in the nature of their sport, but also in competitive nature of this sport, which may introduce confound for differences found between the groups."
    },
    {
      "predicate": "iadas:perspectives",
      "object": "Health pursuits for male should emphasize achievement and function rather than appearance if they are to avoid deleterious consesquences."
